(740473, 77701, 18191, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on your yellow!<br/> <br/>A few things of thought from me.<br/> <br/>There is a round stop right of the glass that could be cleaned up with a quick clone. (I know this was basic editing, but if you are thinking about improving/making a print.)<br/> <br/>Although it is not so relevant in the larger photo, from a thumbnail perspective you can see that the horizon is fairly skewed. So try tilting right to get everything nice and straight.<br/> <br/>I love the colors, and the subject, and I am glad you didn''t use expensive film :). Great message that shows to me those who I know on this site that have never used film in their entire life that they are in fact part of the new generation of photography.<br/> <br/>My last piece of criticism is that the actualy horizon line, where the thickness is a little bit thicker, is a little too distracting. Once again for improvements for your image, you could have clones thi out or blurred it slightly, just to direct less attention to it.<br/> <br/>Once again, congratulations on a great shot and keep it up!<br/> <br/>Lee</td>', 'Congratulations on your yellow! A few things of thought from me. There is a round stop right of the glass that could be cleaned up with a quick clone. (I know this was basic editing, but if you are thinking about improving/making a print.) Although it is not so relevant in the larger photo, from a thumbnail perspective you can see that the horizon is fairly skewed. So try tilting right to get everything nice and straight. I love the colors, and the subject, and I am glad you didn''t use expensive film :). Great message that shows to me those who I know on this site that have never used film in their entire life that they are in fact part of the new generation of photography. My last piece of criticism is that the actualy horizon line, where the thickness is a little bit thicker, is a little too distracting. Once again for improvements for your image, you could have clones thi out or blurred it slightly, just to direct less attention to it. Once again, congratulations on a great shot and keep it up! Lee', '2004-05-28 08:40:06.000000', NULL, 0),
